PAGES=[page('079_page_1.jpg',181,[heading('Remember'),paragraph('If R is the midpoint of the line segment PQ, then, m₁ = m₂ and the coordinates of the midpoint R of the line segment PQ are:'),eq(r'(x,y)=\left(\frac{x_1+x_2}2,\frac{y_1+y_2}2\right)','A'),paragraph('The coordinates of the point that divides the line segment PQ joining two points P(x₁,y₁) and Q(x₂,y₂) externally in the ratio m₁:m₂ (m₁ or m₂ is negative) are:'),eq(r'(x,y)=\left(\frac{m_1x_2-m_2x_1}{m_1-m_2},\frac{m_1y_2-m_2y_1}{m_1-m_2}\right)','B'),heading('Example 2'),paragraph('Find the coordinates of the point which divides the line segment PQ joining the two points (a) P(1,2) and Q(3,4) in the ratio 5:7. (b) P(3,4) and Q(−6,2) in the ratio 3:−2.'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('a. If R(x,y) is a point that divides the line segment PQ in the ratio 5:7, then, the coordinates of R(x,y) is obtained through result (B):'),eq(r'(x,y)=\left(\frac{m_1x_2+m_2x_1}{m_1+m_2},\frac{m_1y_2+m_2y_1}{m_1+m_2}\right),\quad m_1=5,m_2=7,P(1,2),Q(3,4)'),eq(r'=\left(\frac{5(3)+7(1)}{5+7},\frac{5(4)+7(2)}{5+7}\right)=\left(\frac{11}6,\frac{17}6\right)'),paragraph('b. If R(x,y) is a point that divides the line segment PQ in the ratio 3:−2, then the coordinates of R(x,y) is obtained through result (B):'),eq(r'(x,y)=\left(\frac{m_1x_2+m_2x_1}{m_1+m_2},\frac{m_1y_2+m_2y_1}{m_1+m_2}\right)'),eq(r'=\left(\frac{3(-6)+(-2)(3)}{3-2},\frac{3(2)+(-2)(4)}{3-2}\right)=(-24,-2),\quad m_1=3,m_2=-2'),note('The box mixes two ratio conventions. With positive external lengths p:q, use (pQ−qP)/(p−q), p ≠ q. With signed weights m₁:m₂, use (m₁Q+m₂P)/(m₁+m₂), m₁+m₂ ≠ 0. Example 2 correctly uses the latter with 3:−2; its reference to (B) in the internal part should refer to the internal formula. Do not insert a negative weight into the external-minus formula a second time.'),heading('7.1.3 The medians and angle bisectors of a triangle are concurrent'),heading('i. The medians of a triangle are concurrent'),paragraph('Proof: If A(x₁,y₁), B(x₂,y₂) and C(x₃,y₃) are the vertices of a triangle ABC and P, Q and R are the midpoints of the sides AB, BC and CA, then, the coordinates of the midpoint Q through mid point formula.'),eq(r'Q\left(\frac{x_2+x_3}2,\frac{y_2+y_3}2\right),\quad m_1=m_2'),graph(4,'medians'),paragraph('If G(x,y) is the centroid (in centre) of the triangle ABC, then, the coordinates of the point G that divides the median AQ in the ratio m₁:m₂ = 2:1 are:'),eq(r'G(x,y)=\left(\frac{2((x_2+x_3)/2)+x_1}{2+1},\frac{2((y_2+y_3)/2)+y_1}{2+1}\right)=\left(\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}3,\frac{y_1+y_2+y_3}3\right)','i'),paragraph('Similarly, the coordinates of the point G(x,y) that divides the medians BR and CP each in the ratio 2:1 are respectively:')]),page('079_page_2.jpg',182,[eq(r'G(x,y)=\left(\frac{2((x_1+x_3)/2)+x_2}{2+1},\frac{2((y_1+y_3)/2)+y_2}{2+1}\right)=\left(\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}3,\frac{y_1+y_2+y_3}3\right)','ii'),eq(r'G(x,y)=\left(\frac{2((x_1+x_2)/2)+x_3}{2+1},\frac{2((y_1+y_2)/2)+y_3}{2+1}\right)=\left(\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}3,\frac{y_1+y_2+y_3}3\right)','iii'),paragraph('Therefore, the point G(x,y) lies on each median and consequently the medians of the triangle ABC are concurrent.'),heading('Example 3'),paragraph('Find the centroid of the triangle ABC, whose vertices are A(3,−5), B(−7,4) and C(10,−2).'),heading('Solution'),paragraph('Let A(3,−5), B(−7,4) and C(10,−2) are the vertices of the triangle ABC, If G(x,y) is the centroid of the triangle ABC; then, the coordinates of the point G(x,y) are:'),eq(r'G(x,y)=\left(\frac{x_1+x_2+x_3}3,\frac{y_1+y_2+y_3}3\right)=\left(\frac{3-7+10}3,\frac{-5+4-2}3\right)=(2,-1)'),heading('ii. The bisectors of a triangle are concurrent'),paragraph('Proof: If ABC is a triangle with vertices A(x₁,y₁), B(x₂,y₂) and C(x₃,y₃), whose lengths are |AB| = c, |BC| = a and |CA| = b, then, the position vectors of A, B and C are respectively:'),eq(r'r_1=(x_1,y_1)=x_1î+y_1ĵ,\quad r_2=(x_2,y_2)=x_2î+y_2ĵ,\quad r_3=(x_3,y_3)=x_3î+y_3ĵ'),paragraph('Consider AD, BE and CF are the internal bisectors of the angles A, B and C that meet at centroid G. This is shown in Figure 7.5.'),graph(5,'bisectors'),paragraph('If AD is the internal bisector of angle A, then:'),eq(r'\frac{BD}{DC}=\frac{BA}{AC}\ \mathrm{or}\ \frac{BD}{DC}=\frac cb\ \Rightarrow\ BD:DC=c:b','i'),paragraph('This means that D divides BC internally in the ratio c:b and the position vector of D is therefore:'),eq(r'\frac{cr_3+br_2}{c+b}'),eq(r'\mathrm{Again},\ \frac{BD}c=\frac{DC}b=\frac{BD+DC}{c+b}=\frac a{c+b}\ \Rightarrow\ BD=\frac{ac}{c+b}','ii'),paragraph('If BG is the internal bisector of the angle B, then,'),eq(r'\frac{DG}{AG}=\frac{BD}{AB}=\frac{ac/(b+c)}c=\frac a{b+c}\ \Rightarrow\ DG:GA=a:(b+c)'),paragraph('The position vector of G(x,y) is:'),eq(r'r=\frac{ar_1+(b+c)((cr_3+br_2)/(b+c))}{a+b+c}=\frac{ar_1+br_2+cr_3}{a+b+c}=\frac{a(x_1,y_1)+b(x_2,y_2)+c(x_3,y_3)}{a+b+c}','iii'),note('The medians meet at the centroid, whereas the internal angle bisectors meet at the incenter. The source calls both G and incorrectly uses “centroid (in centre)” or “centroid” in these passages; they are generally different points. For a nondegenerate triangle all side lengths a,b,c are positive. In triangle ABD the angle-bisector theorem yields AG:GD = AB:BD, equivalent to the displayed DG:AG; the common point is the incenter. Its coordinate formula continues on page 183.')])]
